questions.solution_text:
An edge leaves the start vertex for every activity with no immediate predecessors.
$A$, $B$ and $C$ each show a dash in the predecessor column.
$3$ edges leave the start vertex.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{midarrow/.style={decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\arrow{>}}}, postaction={decorate}}}
\coordinate (S) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (2.4,2.0);
\coordinate (Q) at (2.4,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2.4,-2.0);
\coordinate (M) at (5.0,1.0);
\coordinate (Z) at (7.6,0);
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (P) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$A,\,2$};
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (Q) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$B,\,8$};
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (R) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$C,\,5$};
\draw[midarrow] (P) -- (M) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$D,\,3$};
\draw[midarrow] (Q) -- (M) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$E,\,6$};
\draw[midarrow] (R) -- (Z) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$F,\,4$};
\draw[midarrow] (M) -- (Z) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$G,\,1$};
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (M) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Z) circle (2.5pt);
\node[font=\small, left] at (S) {Start};
\node[font=\small, right] at (Z) {Finish};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The predecessor column for $G$ lists $E$ and $F$.
An activity begins at the vertex where all of its immediate predecessors end.
$G$ begins where $E$ and $F$ end.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{midarrow/.style={decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\arrow{>}}}, postaction={decorate}}}
\coordinate (S) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (2.2,1.6);
\coordinate (Q) at (2.2,-1.6);
\coordinate (R) at (4.6,1.6);
\coordinate (T) at (4.6,-1.6);
\coordinate (U) at (6.8,0);
\coordinate (Z) at (8.8,0);
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (P) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$A,\,9$};
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (Q) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$B,\,4$};
\draw[midarrow] (P) -- (R) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$C,\,7$};
\draw[midarrow] (Q) -- (T) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$D,\,2$};
\draw[midarrow] (R) -- (U) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$E,\,5$};
\draw[midarrow] (T) -- (U) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$F,\,3$};
\draw[midarrow] (U) -- (Z) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$G,\,6$};
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (T) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (U) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Z) circle (2.5pt);
\node[font=\small, left] at (S) {Start};
\node[font=\small, right] at (Z) {Finish};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$D$ needs $\{A, B\}$ and $E$ needs $\{B, C\}$.
The two lists share $B$ only, so $B$ cannot end at the same vertex as $A$ or as $C$.
One dummy carries $B$ forward to the vertex where $D$ starts, and a second carries $B$ forward to the vertex where $E$ starts.
$2$ dummy activities are needed.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{decorations.markings}
\tikzset{midarrow/.style={decoration={markings, mark=at position 0.5 with {\arrow{>}}}, postaction={decorate}}}
\coordinate (S) at (0,0);
\coordinate (P) at (2.4,2.0);
\coordinate (Q) at (2.4,0);
\coordinate (R) at (2.4,-2.0);
\coordinate (M) at (5.0,2.0);
\coordinate (N) at (5.0,-2.0);
\coordinate (Z) at (7.4,0);
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (P) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$A,\,6$};
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (Q) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$B,\,2$};
\draw[midarrow] (S) -- (R) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$C,\,8$};
\draw[midarrow, dashed] (Q) -- (P) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0$} node[pos=0.75, right, font=\small] {dummy};
\draw[midarrow, dashed] (Q) -- (R) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$0$} node[pos=0.75, right, font=\small] {dummy};
\draw[midarrow] (P) -- (M) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$D,\,4$};
\draw[midarrow] (R) -- (N) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$E,\,9$};
\draw[midarrow] (M) -- (Z) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$F,\,3$};
\draw[midarrow] (N) -- (Z) node[pos=0.35, fill=white, inner sep=1pt] {$G,\,5$};
\fill (S) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (P) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (M) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (N) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (Z) circle (2.5pt);
\node[font=\small, left] at (S) {Start};
\node[font=\small, right] at (Z) {Finish};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$C$ has predecessor list $\{A\}$ and $D$ has predecessor list $\{A, B\}$.
They share $A$ but not $B$, so they cannot start from the same vertex.
A dummy is drawn from the end of $A$ to the end of $B$, giving $D$ a start vertex reached by both while $C$ still starts from $A$ alone.
[tikz]
